 i see you\'re here for back pain .\n[patient] yes .\n[doctor] hey dragon , i\'m seeing mrs. lee today for , uh , back pain . she\'s a 40-year-old female . so can you tell me what happened to you ?\n[patient] yes . i was at a birthday party and it was surprise party . so we were all like crunched behind and hiding . and when the person came in we all jumped out , and then i fell down the little stair and i rammed my back into the railing of the s- , uh , staircase .\n[doctor] so it was a surprise for you too ?\n[patient] yes it was .\n[doctor] very good . okay . so , um , did it hurt because you hit it , or did you twist ? what happened when you ?\n[patient] i think it was my motion-\n[doctor] okay .\n[patient] . of jumping up , or something .\n[doctor] okay .\n[patient] and maybe a twisting .\n[doctor] okay . any pain shooting down your legs at all ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] is it on one side or the other of your back ?\n[patient] um , it\'s more on the right .\n[doctor] on the right . is there anything that makes it better or worse ?\n[patient] uh , if i\'m lying flat it hurts a little more , and any twisting motion for sure .\n[doctor] okay . all right . and do you have any history or any back problems before ?\n[patient] i did . i had a fusion done .\n[doctor] okay , yeah that lumbar fusion . um , let\'s take an x-ray . hey . or let\'s look at an x-ray . hey dragon , show me the latest x-ray . okay , we can see where you had your fusion , t4 here on the x-ray . but i do n\'t see anything that looks , um , significantly abnormal . it does n\'t appear to have any compression fractures or anything like that . so , it\'s essentially , except for the fusion , a normal exam . let me do your exam for you here . can i have you stand up please ? does it hurt when i twist you that way ?\n[patient] yes .\n[doctor] okay . you can sit back down . if i lift your leg here , does that bother you ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] how \'bout when i lift this leg ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] any pain shooting down your legs when i do that ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] okay . and can you feel me touching down here .\n[patient] yes .\n[doctor] okay , great . so , you had a injury to your back when you were twisting at the birthday party . you do n\'t really have any pain or numbness down your legs . you have some stiffness with rotation , um , but you do n\'t have any evidence of a , um , what we would call a pinched nerved or radiculopathy . so , i think you basically have a strain of your lower back . um , have you been taking any medications ?\n[patient] just some ibuprofen .\n[doctor] okay . and do you have any other , um , medical problems ?\n[patient] um i take medication for anxiety .\n[doctor] okay . and that\'s all ? okay . um , so what we\'re gon na do is i\'m gon na give you , um , just continue the motrin at 800mg , three times a day , with food . i want you to use some ice and heat , alternating on your back . but i do want you walking .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] um , and , uh , this should sort of take care of itself after a few days . if it\'s not , or it\'s getting worse , i want you to come back and see me over the next week .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] does that sound good ?\n[patient] it does .\n[doctor] okay , dragon . hey dragon , go ahead and order the medications and procedures and close out the note . come on i\'ll bring you out to check out .\n[patient] thank you .\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMrs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of back pain. The onset of her pain began when she was twisting at a birthday party. She states she was surprised and jumped up, fell down the stairs, and rammed her back into the railing. She denies any pain shooting down her legs. She also denies any history of back problems before her lumbar fusion. She states that the pain is worse when she lies flat and with twisting motions. She also reports that she has a history of anxiety and is taking medication for it.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the back shows no evidence of a pinched nerve or radiculopathy. Pain with rotation. No pain with leg lift.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine obtained today show no evidence of compression fractures or significant abnormalities, except for the previous lumbar fusion.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nStrain of the back.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current symptoms. I have recommended that she continue to take Motrin 800 mg 3 times a day with food. She is to use ice and heat alternating on her back, and I want her to walk. She will follow up with me over the next week if her symptoms do not improve or if they worsen. All questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me over the next week if her symptoms do not improve or if they worsen. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMrs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of back pain. The onset of her pain began when she was jumping out from behind a couch at a surprise birthday party. She reports that she rammed her back into the railing of the stairs. She denies any pain radiating down her legs. The pain is located on the right side of her back and is worse with twisting and lying flat. She has a history of a lumbar fusion. She reports that she has been taking ibuprofen for pain. She also reports that she takes medication for her anxiety. She denies any numbness or tingling.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports back pain.\nNeurological: Denies numbness or tingling.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the musculoskeletal system: There is stiffness with rotation. Examination of the neurological system is unremarkable.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine, obtained in office today, reveal a fusion at T4. No acute findings.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Back strain.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nHistory of lumbar fusion.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that she had a surprise birthday party.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nIbuprofen.\nAnxiety medication.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nAnxiety medication.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nContinue taking Motrin 800 mg, three times a day, with food. Use ice and heat alternating on her back. She should continue to walk. If her symptoms do not improve or worsen, she should follow up with me within the next week.\n\nIMMS\n\nMotrin 800 mg, 3 times a day, with food.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCC:\n\nBack pain.\n\nHPI:\n\nMs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for an evaluation of back pain. She states she was at a birthday party and she ran her back into the railing of the staircase and twisted her back. She denies any pain radiating down her legs. Lying flat and twisting motions aggravate the pain. She is currently taking ibuprofen.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS:\n\nIbuprofen.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY:\n\nAnxiety.\n\nPAST SURGICAL HISTORY:\n\nLumbar fusion\n\nEXAM\n\nExamination of the back shows range of motion without pain. Straight leg raise is negative. Sensation is intact.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine show no obvious signs of acute fracture. Evidence of a prior lumbar fusion with hardware in good position.\n\nIMPRESSION\n\nLumbar spine strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this point, I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. I have recommended ice and heat. She will continue with Motrin 800 mg 3 times a day with food. She will follow up with me as needed.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], request_timeout=60)[0m


