as needed 30 to 45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant . she can also use zofran if needed . however , i have encouraged her to reduce greasy and fatty foods and alcohol consumption . i have asked her to follow up in one month by emailing over mychart regarding any side effects and issues , or issues with elavil or if adjustments are needed .\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up for IBS with functional abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBrittany Edwards is a 32-year-old female who was last seen in 08/2019 with flares of abdominal pain, who was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) and treated with Bentyl. She had a previous prescription for Reglan that she received from the emergency room that she used as needed for nausea. She was instructed to start a low-dose fiber supplement, such as Citrucel, daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, Bentyl up to 4 times daily for intestinal cramping, and to stop Reglan and use Zofran as needed. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been going out to eat on Friday nights and Saturday nights and has had some spicy foods or spicy shrimp. She states that her gut does not always appreciate this. She notes that she is starting to notice bowel movements between 2-3 times a day on normal days. She denies having nausea on normal days, but states that she does have nausea after she has gone out. She denies diarrhea on normal days, but states that she does have diarrhea after she goes out. She states that she has been avoiding heavy foods. She states that she had fried chicken today and it was fine.\n\nThe patient reports that she drinks tequila, Patron, and dark liquor. She states that she only gets 2 drinks every now and then, and occasionally drinks a martini. She states that she has been drinking water instead of soda.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been having cramping and sharp pains. She states that she has been avoiding heavy foods. She states that she ate pasta with chicken alfredo last night, which she usually does not have an issue with. She states that she has not had any issues with the chicken.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nIrritable bowel syndrome.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that she goes out to eat on Friday nights and Saturday nights.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nBentyl 20 mg as needed 30-45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGeneral\nPleasant, stated age.\n\nCardiovascular\nRegular rate and rhythm.\n\nRespiratory\nClear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nGastrointestinal\nSoft, non-tender. No masses or organomegaly. Normal active bowel sounds.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBrittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female with IBS and functional abdominal pain, aggravated by high stress, poor eating habits, and poor sleeping habits. She is doing well today.\n\nDiagnosis\nIrritable bowel syndrome (IBS) with functional abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting.\n\nI have encouraged dietary and lifestyle modifications, as well as starting a neuromodulator such as Elavil 25 mg at bedtime for IBS. She can use Bentyl 20 mg as needed 30-45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran if needed. However, I have encouraged her to reduce greasy and fatty foods and alcohol consumption. I have asked her to follow up in 1 month by email regarding any side effects and issues, or issues with Elavil or if adjustments are needed.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 1 month by email. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nDiarrhea, nausea, and abdominal pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBrittany Edwards is a 32-year-old female who was last seen in August of 2019 with flares of abdominal pain, who was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) and treated with Bentyl. She had a previous prescription for Reglan that she received from the emergency room that she used as needed for nausea. She was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement, such as Citrucel, daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, Bentyl up to four times daily for intestinal cramping, and stop Reglan and use Zofran as needed. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been going out to eat on Friday nights and Saturday nights and that she has had some spicy foods or spicy shrimp. She notes that she has been having 2 to 3 bowel movements per day on normal days. She reports that she has been having diarrhea after she eats out. She states that she has been avoiding heavy foods. She notes that she has been doing better when she drinks water instead of soda.\n\nShe reports that she is now having cramping and sharp pains.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nReglan\n• Frequency: PRN\n• Reason: Nausea\n\nBentyl\n• Frequency: PRN\n• Reason: Intestinal cramping\n\nZofran\n• Frequency: PRN\n• Reason: Nausea\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, or changes in appetite or weight.\n• Gastrointestinal: Reports diarrhea, nausea, and abdominal pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm.\n• Gastrointestinal: Soft, non-tender abdomen with normal bowel sounds.\n\nRESULTS\n\nN/A\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBrittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female with IBS and functional abdominal pain, aggravated by high stress, poor eating habits, and poor sleeping habits. Dietary and lifestyle modifications were encouraged. She will start a neuromodulator as needed. She can use Bentyl as needed 30 to 45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran if needed. She should reduce greasy and fatty foods and alcohol consumption.\n\nThe patient is to follow up in 1 month by email through MyChart regarding any side effects and issues, or issues with Elavil or if adjustments are needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 month by email through MyChart regarding any side effects and issues, or issues with Elavil or if adjustments are needed.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\n• IBS, treated with Bentyl.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nReglan\n• Frequency: PRN\n• Reason: Nausea\n\nBentyl\n• Frequency: PRN\n• Reason: Intestinal cramping\n\nZofran\n• Frequency: PRN\n• Reason: Nausea\n\nPHYSICAL DESCRIPTION\n\nMs. Edwards is a pleasant, alert and cooperative 32-year-old female.\n\nVITALS\n\nN/A\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMEDICAL\n\nIBS, treated with Bentyl.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nReglan\n• Frequency: PRN\n• Reason: Nausea\n\nBentyl\n• Frequency: PRN\n• Reason: Intestinal cramping\n\nZofran\n• Frequency: PRN\n• Reason: Nausea\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 month by email through MyChart regarding any side effects and issues, or issues with Elavil or if adjustments are needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up irritable bowel syndrome (IBS).\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Brittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who returns in follow up for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) with functional abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.\n\nMs. Edwards was last seen in August 2019 with flares of abdominal pain and was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome that was treated with Bentyl. She had a previous prescription for Reglan that she received from the emergency room that she used as needed for nausea. She was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement such as Citrucel daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, and Bentyl up to 4 times daily for intestinal cramping. She was instructed to discontinue Reglan and to use Zofran as needed for nausea. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.\n\nMs. Edwards continues to have intermittent abdominal symptoms. Monday through Thursday she has less flare ups of her symptoms but states she is currently having cramps and sharp pains. She describes she recently ate shrimp which caused her to have both upper and lower abdominal pain and bloating. However, she notices episodes of diarrhea after eating chicken alfredo and consuming alcohol. She does not believe the alfredo contributes to her symptoms. The patient states she consumes 2 drinks when out of either tequila or brown liquor and occasionally a martini. She does her best to avoid “heavy foods,” but ate fried chicken today without any symptoms. She has stopped drinking sodas which has been helpful.\u202fThe patient reports passing 2-3 formed stools a day.\n\nREVIEW OF SYMPTOMS\n\n•\u202fGastrointestinal:\u202fPositive nausea, diarrhea, and abdominal pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nAlert and cooperative, appears stated age.\n\nRespiratory\nLungs clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\nHeart regular rate and rhythm.\n\nGastrointestinal\nAbdomen soft, non-tender, no masses or organomegaly. Normoactive bowel sounds.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Irritable bowel syndrome\n\nBrittany Edwards is a 32-year-old established patient who returned today to discuss management of IBS and functional abdominal pain made worse by high stress, poor eating habits, and poor sleeping habits.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have encouraged dietary and lifestyle modifications as well as starting a neuromodulator, such as Elavil 25 mg at bedtime for IBS. She can use\u202fBentyl\u202f20 mg as needed 30-45 minutes\u202fbefore eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran as needed for nausea. However, I have encouraged her to reduce greasy fatty foods and alcohol consumption.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nSend MyChart message in 1 month regarding any side effects of issues with Elavil or if dose adjustments are needed.\nReturn to clinic in 1 month or sooner if needed.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], request_timeout=60)[0m


