 have any , um , chest tightness or pressure or any shortness of breath ?\n[patient] yeah i\'m taking the full dose of that medicine too , and no none of those symptoms .\n[doctor] okay . anything else i should be aware of or that we should be checking ?\n[patient] mm , no .\n[doctor] okay , good . are you doing the blood pressure checks at home ?\n[patient] yes . yes i am .\n[doctor] okay good . what kind of numbers are you seeing ?\n[patient] well , in the morning i\'m seeing lower numbers than in the evening , um , where i run anywhere from like 130 to 145 or 150 over 95 to 100 on the bottom .\n[doctor] okay .\n[patient] but in the evenings if i sit there for a while and then take it , it goes down . kind of like when i come here it\'s always high but then it goes down . in the mornings it\'s generally more normal where it\'s supposed to be .\n[doctor] uh- .\n[patient] mm , also i\'m taking my medicine in the morning when i get up instead of in the evening .\n[doctor] yeah , so it sounds like we are not too far out of where we wan na be .\n[patient] right , mm-hmm .\n[doctor] okay . well let me get a listen to your heart , go ahead and hop up here .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] use my general exam template . all right , go ahead and take some deep breaths . okay , good . any fever , cough , shortness of breath or sore throat ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] any recent travel or contact or exposure to the coronavirus ?\n[patient] nope .\n[doctor] have you received your coronavirus vaccine ?\n[patient] yes , on february 9th .\n[doctor] okay , great . all right well i\'m just going to look at your ankles and feet , i\'m looking for any signs of swelling .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] all right , well everything looks good . you can go ahead and sit up miss taylor . all right , so the good news is everything is looking okay . i think we both agree if you were able to stop drinking your blood pressure would cease to be an issue for you .\n[patient] yeah i agree with that .\n[doctor] so let us discuss the potential medicine to help you with that . what it does is it blocks some of the brain receptors that alcohol stimulates and it tricks the brain into thinking it does not need alcohol , thereby cutting down the urges .\n[patient] the urge to drink or the constant thinking about it ?\n[doctor] it actually will help with both .\n[patient] okay . so is it like a one-time thing ?\n[doctor] generally the shot is given about once a month , it can also be given as a daily pill , but the shot does tend to work a bit better .\n[patient] yeah i\'ve taken things like this in the past and if it is a pill , i know i wo n\'t take it . with a shot , it\'s not like you can un-take it .\n[doctor] yeah that\'s true . and the shot has been shown to work , it is more just a matter of getting you in to get your shot a month later with the nurse .\n[patient] yeah , sure , sure .\n[doctor] okay , so it\'s called naltrexone . i think the other name might be vivitrol . like i\'ve said , the research on it is pretty good .\n[patient] i think i may have taken the naltrexone before .\n[doctor] okay , yeah , maybe you have .\n[patient] yeah , if it was available in a pill form then i\'m almost certain i\'ve tried it b- before .\n[doctor] okay . if this sounds good though , we can go ahead get you started with the shot . i am looking and it looks like we have you coming back in on may 17th for your first shot , does that sound good ?\n[patient] yeah , yeah , that sounds great . are there any possible side effects ?\n[doctor] yeah , some people will experience some nausea , headache , dizziness , anxi- anxiety , tiredness and trouble sleeping , but generally the side effects are mild and should go away within a few days .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] then we can schedule you for a follow-up in june to see how you are doing .\n[patient] all right that sounds good .\n[doctor] all right great . well otherwise stay with your current medicines , keep checking your blood pressure , and let\'s see if we can stop the alcohol for good . let us know if you have any questions or any issues come up after you receive the first dose .\n[patient] all right , sounds good , thank you .\n[doctor] you are welcome , have a good rest of your day .\n[patient] you too .\n[doctor] miss taylor is drinking alcohol four to five days per week to excess . no tobacco , no substance abuse , skin is moist , good trigger . on exam she is alert , pleasant , in no acute distress . she is not diabetic , there is no tremor , no cva tenderness , chest clear to percussion and auscultation , unlabored breathing , cardiac rhythm regular , no murmur , no gallop , jvp flat , with a head at 90 degrees . she does not appear anxious , agitated or depressed . my impression , alcohol use disorder . she would like to start naltrexone which we have previously discussed . she will take the injectable form and start on may 17th . we reviewed side effects and risks . the potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to her for many reasons . hypertension , she is taking lisinopril 10 milligrams daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 milligrams daily . quite possibly her blood pressure will improve completely with cessation of alcohol . her target bp is 130 to 140 over 70 to 80 . i will see her back week of june 14th , which is likely around the time of her second naltrexone injection . i recommend upgrading influenza vaccination and shingrix . number two , she did defer this .\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up for hypertension.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAmanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. She has a history of significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04/16/2021, her blood pressure was 130/90. She presented today at 150/100 and was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily, lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been doing well. She states that she has been struggling with not drinking. She wanted to discuss this shot that I had mentioned last time, but we did not get to discuss it too much.\n\nShe reports that she last had a drink on Monday. She states that she has gone a couple of days without it. She has been drinking for a while. She states that she drinks four to five times a week with hard alcohol. She notes that she is taking half of the lisinopril dose due to lethargy and fatigue the first time she took it. She states that she felt much better and did not have the headaches or the "wooshing" that started in her left ear. She states that it definitely helped but she does not know if it will be okay until she stops drinking.\n\nShe is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She denies any chest tightness or shortness of breath.\n\nShe states that she is doing blood pressure checks at home. She reports that in the morning she is seeing lower numbers than in the evening. She states that she is running anywhere from 130 to 145/95 to 100 on the bottom. She states that in the evenings, if she sits there for a while and then takes it, it goes down. She states that in the mornings it is generally more normal where it is supposed to be. She notes that she is taking her medicine in the morning when she gets up instead of in the evening.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nMild hypercholesterolemia.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nShe denies tobacco use. She denies any substance abuse. She states that she received her COVID-19 vaccine on 02/09/2021.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nNot provided.\n\nALLERGIES\n\nNot provided.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nLisinopril 10 mg daily.\nHydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological\n- Mental Status: Alert, pleasant, in no acute distress.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Pulse: Regular rate and rhythm.\n- Blood Pressure: BP is 150/100.\n- Peripheral Pulses: Normal capillary refill, no edema.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation: Unlabored breathing, clear to percussion and auscultation bilaterally.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: No tenderness to palpation.\n\nVital Signs\n- Blood Pressure: 150/100.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAlcohol use disorder.\n- Medical Reasoning: She reports drinking alcohol 4-5 days per week to excess.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the potential medication to help her with this. She would like to start naltrexone which we have previously discussed. She will take the injectable form and start on 05/17/2021. We reviewed side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to her for many reasons. Her hypertension is controlled with lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Possibly her blood pressure will improve completely with cessation of alcohol. Her target BP is 130-140/70-80.\n- Medical Treatment: She will take the injectable form of naltrexone starting 05/17/2021. She will follow up in the week of 06/14/2021, which is likely around the time of her second naltrexone injection.\n\nHypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: She is doing well on her current regimen.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily.\n\nImmunizations.\n- Medical Reasoning: She is due for an influenza and shingles vaccination.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I recommended that she get these updated. She deferred.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHypertension.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAmanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. The patient\'s history is significant for alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04/16/2021, her blood pressure was 130/90. Today, she presents at 150/100. She was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nThe patient reports she is struggling with not drinking. She wanted to discuss the shot that was mentioned at the last visit, but they did not get to discuss it too much.\n\nThe patient states she has been drinking on Monday. She reports drinking 4-5 times a week, hard alcohol. She had been drinking for a while. She is taking half of the lisinopril. She reports she felt lethargic and tired the first time she took it so she only took half of the dose the next time. She feels much better and she does not have the headaches or the "swoshing" that started in her left ear. She feels like it is definitely helping but she does not know it is not going to be okay until she stops drinking.\n\nShe is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest tightness, pressure, or shortness of breath. She is doing blood pressure checks at home. In the morning, she sees lower numbers than in the evening. She reports blood pressure readings ranging from 130-145 over 95-100 in the morning and 130-150 over 100 in the evening. She also reports that her blood pressure is always high when she comes to the clinic but then it goes down. She is taking her medications in the morning. She states it sounds like we are not too far out of where we want to be.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever, cough, shortness of breath, or chills.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, pressure, or dyspnea on exertion.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Neurological: Denies tremor.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation, unlabored breathing.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rhythm, no murmurs, no gallop, JVP flat at 90 degrees.\n• Musculoskeletal: No edema.\n\nVITALS\n\n• Blood pressure: 150/100\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAmanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. The patient\'s history is significant for alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04/16/2021, her blood pressure was 130/90. Today, she presents at 150/100. She was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nThe patient reports she is struggling with not drinking. She wanted to discuss the shot that was mentioned at the last visit, but they did not get to discuss it too much.\n\nThe patient states she has been drinking on Monday. She reports drinking 4-5 times a week, hard alcohol. She had been drinking for a while. She is taking half of the lisinopril. She reports she felt lethargic and tired the first time she took it so she only took half of the dose the next time. She feels much better and she does not have the headaches or the "swoshing" that started in her left ear. She feels like it is definitely helping but she does not know it is not going to be okay until she stops drinking.\n\nShe is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest tightness, pressure, or shortness of breath. She is doing blood pressure checks at home. In the morning, she sees lower numbers than in the evening. She reports blood pressure readings ranging from 130-145 over 95-100 in the morning and 130-150 over 100 in the evening. She also reports that her blood pressure is always high when she comes to the clinic but then it goes down. She is taking her medications in the morning. She states it sounds like we are not too far out of where we want to be.\n\nThe patient is doing well today. She is doing well with her blood pressure medications. She is currently drinking 4-5 times a week to excess. She is not taking the lisinopril as prescribed. She is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest pain, shortness of breath, or other cardiac symptoms.\n\nThe patient would like to start naltrexone. We previously discussed this. She will take the injectable form and start on 05/17/2021. We reviewed the side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to her for many reasons. Hypertension, she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Quite possibly her blood pressure will improve completely with cessation of alcohol. Her target BP is 130-140/70-80. She will be seen back week of 06/14/2021, which is likely around the time of her second naltrexone injection. I recommended upgrading influenza vaccination and Shingrix. Number 2, she did defer this.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nContinue her current medications. She will follow up with me in June.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nContinue her current medications. She will follow up with me in June.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY\n\nSignificant alcohol use disorder. Mild hypercholesterolemia.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nHydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily.\nLisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports drinking alcohol 4-5 times per week to excess.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nNone pertinent.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever, cough, shortness of breath, or chills.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain, pressure, or dyspnea on exertion.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nNeurological: Denies tremor.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation, unlabored breathing.\nCardiovascular: Regular rhythm, no murmurs, no gallop, JVP flat at 90 degrees.\nMusculoskeletal: No edema.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 150/100\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Hypertension.\n• Alcohol use disorder.\n• Hyperlipidemia.\n\nPLAN\n\nAmanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. The patient\'