e other wash , when i would use it twice a day , i was drying out . but not with this one .\n[doctor] i see . okay . well then do what feels good . if you do notice that you\'re starting to get dry , then reduce to once per day .\n[patient] that sounds good .\n[doctor] so to review , um , continue using the same face wash , continue with metro cream , and increase the use of doxycycline to twice a day .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] and then just do it until it\'s gone , and then do it for once a day for another week before stopping .\n[patient] okay , sounds good .\n[doctor] all right . and i\'ll rewrite your instructions here .\n[patient] thank you so much .\n[doctor] if there are any other questions or you\'re getting different instructions , feel free to reach out to me and we\'ll clarify , okay ?\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] but in general , doxycycline is such a low risk . we\'re not gon na have you on this forever . but especially since we\'re adding the tacrolimus , i expect it will clear quickly .\n[patient] sounds good .\n[doctor] all right . and i just wan na really help knock it out as fast as possible , since you\'ve been dealing with it for so long now . i would take it twice a day until it\'s gone , and then once a day for a week before you stop . i\'ll write it out like that . so twice a day until rash is gone , and then once a day for another week before stopping .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] once the rash stops , you can stop the tacrolimus ointment . um , continue doxycycline another week and then stop . but you can keep using the wash and the metro cream .\n[patient] okay , that sounds good . i can do that .\n[doctor] and then hold off until i see you again before you start back any of your old products . um , if it\'s starting to flair again , um , if it\'s just a little bit , you can try just doing the tacrolimus for a couple days , to see if that\'s enough . if it\'s not , then restart the dy- doxycycline as well .\n[patient] all right .\n[doctor] i do n\'t expect that to happen , but , you know , if it does , you have the tools . um , then you\'ll be seeing me for a followup . we can figure out what to do next . i would say if it\'s getting worse rather than better , just give me a call or send me a mychart message , okay ?\n[patient] yeah , sounds good .\n[doctor] do you have enough refills ?\n[patient] yeah , i think so .\n[doctor] okay . so it looks like you have another refill of the doxycycline , so you\'re good there . metro cream , you probably still have enough of that one .\n[patient] yes , i do .\n[doctor] all right . and i\'ll cancel the elidel cream , just so we do n\'t complicate your list . uh , you have plenty of the face wash still .\n[patient] yes .\n[doctor] okay . uh , and we\'ll get rid of the clindamycin from your list as well .\n[patient] and what would you suggest for a moisturizer ?\n[doctor] yeah , so for that i would do something really basic like vanicream . or even , if it\'s just really dry , you could use a little bit of petroleum jelly . um , i\'ll give you a couple samples , okay ? let\'s make sure we have a followup in about four weeks .\n[patient] okay , thank you .\n[doctor] okay . and i would stay away from any kind of anti-aging thing , any kind of plant thing . um , really let\'s just be sure we talk about any new products , or reach out to me if you\'re gon na try something else . um , let\'s go get those samples .\n[patient] okay , that sounds good .\n[doctor] for assessment and plan , perioral dermatitis , rosacea , including ocular rosacea , and copy and paste her patient instructions . i recommend that she gently wash her eyelids once a day with gentle cleanser such as vanicream , also use bruder mask as needed .\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRash on the face.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 60-year-old female who presents for a follow-up visit for her perioral dermatitis.\n\nThe patient reports that she is doing well. She has a small bit of leftover rash. She started using doxycycline 100 mg once a day because she thinks the pharmacist said to limit it to once a day if it is getting better. She notes that she was taking it twice a day and she did notice a lot of improvement. She then started doing it only once a day and it seems the same and it\'s not continuing to get better. She is not breastfeeding at this time. She stopped breastfeeding and then asked for the doxycycline at that time. The patient did not take the Elidel because when she read about it, she got worried. She wanted to just try the doxycycline and see. The doctor explained that a lot of the things she read about is not actually relevant to the cream or ointment form, but it is about the oral form that is used in really high doses, long-term, after people have a heart transplant or a kidney transplant or something like that. The patient can resume taking the doxycycline twice a day again.\n\nThe patient reports that she is using the sulfacetamide wash twice a day. She is not drying out. She is using the Metrocream after her face wash. She is not using Cetaphil cleanser or Neutrogena wipes. She is not using any lotion right now, just those two. The patient went down to just taking doxycycline once a day last week. The first two weeks, she did twice a day and then she started seeing improvement and then she changed to just once a day.\n\nThe patient is Fitzpatrick skin type IV to V. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few really faint erythematous papules and just a little bit of redness around and underneath the nostrils. She is right, it is not totally gone.\n\nThe doctor would go ahead and go back to twice a day, every day, with the doxycycline.\n\nThe patient was advised that out of known risks associated with medications, topical are usually safer and preferable to oral medications. The doctor explained that doxycycline can give you bad upset stomach or heartburn. It will make you sunburn even if you never sunburn, so you have to protect yourself.\n\nThe patient was advised that if insurance will cover the Elidel, to pick it up and start using it. The patient did check and insurance is not covering it. The doctor stated that it is actually more expensive. She looked at the Eucrisa that the doctor recommended in her notes, and if it is still expensive, she could try that instead. The doctor stated that it is actually more expensive. It is actually more expensive. The prices fluctuate. When she looked before, it was around 30 to $40. When she looked now, it was around $70. The patient would have picked it up if it was around $200, but it was coming to around $850 after insurance. The doctor found the cream form. We could try ointment form.\n\nThe patient was advised that Elidel is not a steroid. It is called a calcineurin inhibitor. It is kinda like a steroid in that it calms inflammation. The doctor stated that it is not a steroid, so do not use steroids on the face, for sure, as they will make this kind of rash worse. Also, steroids carry the risk of causing thinning of the skin. These medications do not cause thinning of the skin and they are not going to cause some other kind of rash. The thing to know is that sometimes 5 to 10 minutes after you put it on, it can cause this weird kind of tingly or needle-like sensation or make it redder or flush. But it should only last a couple of seconds and then go away. It is not an allergy or anything bad. It does not mean it is going to happen again. It will still work, so keep using it as long as you know you can stand it, okay?\n\nThe patient was advised that once the rash stops, she can stop the tacrolimus ointment. Continue doxycycline another week and then stop. She can keep using the face wash and the Metrocream.\n\nThe patient was advised to hold off until she sees her again before she starts back any of her old products. If it is starting to flair again, if it is just a little bit, she can try just doing the tacrolimus for a couple of days to see if that is enough. If it is not, then restart the doxycycline as well.\n\nThe patient was advised that she will be seeing me for a follow-up. We can figure out what to do next. She will be seeing me for a follow-up. We can figure out what to do next. I would say if it is getting worse rather than better, just give me a call or send me a MyChart message, okay?\n\nThe patient was advised that she has another refill of the doxycycline, so she is good there. Metrocream, she probably still has enough of that one.\n\nThe patient was advised that we will get rid of the Clindamycin from her list as well. She has plenty of the face wash still.\n\nThe patient was advised that for that I would do something really basic like Vanicream. Or even if it is just really dry, you could use a little bit of petroleum jelly. I will give her a couple of samples, okay? Let\'s make sure we have a follow-up in about four weeks.\n\nThe patient was advised that I would stay away from any kind of anti-aging thing, any kind of plant thing. Really let\'s just be sure we talk about any new products, or reach out to me if she is going to try something else. Let\'s go get those samples.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nPerioral dermatitis.\n\nPatient Instructions\n\nI recommend that she gently wash her eyelids once a day with a gentle cleanser such as Vanicream. Also use Bruder mask as needed.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nContinue using the same face wash, continue with Metrocream, and increase the use of doxycycline to twice a day. Once the rash stops, she can stop the tacrolimus ointment. Continue doxycycline another week and then stop. She can keep using the face wash and the Metrocream. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nDermatology.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a pleasant 61-year-old female who presents for a follow-up today. She was last seen in clinic in 2 weeks ago. She reports that she is doing well and her rash is doing much better. She notes that she has some residual rash that she started using doxycycline once a day because her pharmacist told her to limit it to once a day. She notes that she was taking it twice a day and noticed improvement, but when she started taking it once a day it plateaued. She denies breastfeeding. The patient stopped breastfeeding and requested doxycycline at that time. She was hesitant to use Elidel because she was concerned about the side effects. The provider explained that the side effects she read about were for the oral form used after a heart or kidney transplant and not for as needed use of the cream or ointment. She notes that she stopped breastfeeding and started taking doxycycline. She is currently using the Sulfacetamide wash twice daily without dryness. She is using the Metro cream after her face wash. She denies using Cetaphil cleanser or Neutrogena wipes. She denies using any lotions. She is not using any other type of lotion. She was going to try Elidel, but insurance is not covering it. She checked and it is $850 with her coupon. She also looked at Eucrisa, which is also expensive.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the skin shows Fitzpatrick skin type IV to V. There are a few faint erythematous papules on the bilateral medial cheeks. There is a little bit of redness around and underneath the nares.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Perioral dermatitis\n• Rosacea, including ocular rosacea\n\nPLAN\n\nI have recommended that the patient continue using the Sulfacetamide wash. I recommended that she apply Metro cream to the rash on her face twice daily until the rash is gone and then once a day for another week before stopping. I advised her to continue doxycycline another week and then stop. I recommended that she continue using the face wash and Metro cream. I also recommended that she try using a moisturizer. I provided her with a few samples of Vanicream. I recommended that she stay away from any anti-aging or plant-based products. I advised 