n 8 out of 10 in severity. The pain can occur at any time, but it is most severe after meals. He has noticed that the pain is associated with fatty foods. He has been following a keto diet for the past 3 months and has lost 25 pounds. The patient denies vomiting but notes that he has had low-grade fevers. He denies any family history of gallbladder disease.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that he is a photographer and enjoys nature photography.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient reports that he is taking medications for his diabetes.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nHENT: Denies chest pain, shortness of breath, or abdominal pain.\nGastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nVital signs are normal. Blood pressure is 128/88 mmHg, respiratory rate is 16, pulse is 68, and oxygen saturation is 99% on room air. Auscultation of the heart reveals regular rate and rhythm, no ectopy, no clicks, or murmurs. Lungs are clear bilaterally. Auscultation of the abdomen reveals bowel sounds present but slightly diminished. The abdomen is soft and nontender but there is slight guarding to the right upper quadrant. There is some pain with palpation in that area. Murphy\'s sign is present but there is no rebound tenderness. No peritoneal signs.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound demonstrates multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. There is also mild thickening of the gallbladder wall.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n* Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with abdominal pain that has been ongoing for approximately 3 weeks. His symptoms are associated with fatty foods. His abdominal ultrasound revealed multiple gallstones and mild thickening of the gallbladder wall.\n* Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the nature of his symptoms and the results of his abdominal ultrasound with the patient. I advised him that given his symptoms have not improved with dietary modifications, it is recommended that he undergo a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. I also advised him that he should avoid fatty foods after surgery and eat foods high in fiber. I recommended that he follow up with his primary care physician for his diabetes.\n* Medical Treatment: We will schedule a laparoscopic cholecystectomy.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 week for postoperative care. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of type 2 diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports enjoying nature photography.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of cholelithiasis. Both of his parents have had cholecystectomies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports low-grade fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports right-sided abdominal pain and nausea. Denies vomiting.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 128/88 mmHg.\nRespiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\nHeart Rate: 68 beats per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 99% on room air.\nBody Temperature: Afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear and equal bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No murmurs, clicks, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Positive slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. Positive for Murphy signs. Peritoneal signs not appreciated.\n- Auscultation: Bowel sounds normal in all 4 quadrants.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound obtained at an outside facility is reviewed today. This demonstrates multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. I do appreciate some mild thickening. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstone