ent options maybe like an epidural steroid injection to help take down some of that inflammation\n[patient] mm-hmm\n[doctor] it might make you feel better but we\'ll we\'ll deal with that when we get get there okay\n[patient] sure\n[doctor] any questions\n[patient] not at this point\n[doctor] okay great thank you\n[patient] thank you\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLower back pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports a history of right knee surgery 2 years ago.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports a history of right knee arthroscopy in 2020.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he moved boxes when his son was moving out of his college dorm and injured his lower back.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports that he has been taking Tylenol and ibuprofen.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports lower back pain, right leg pain, and numbness and tingling in the right foot. Denies weakness in legs.\nNeurological: Reports numbness and tingling in the right foot. Denies bladder or bowel incontinence.\n\nVITALS\n\nTemperature: Afebrile.\nBlood pressure: 117/56.\nHeart rate: 68.\nOxygen saturation: 95% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: Bony protuberance of C5-C6.\nMusculoskeletal: No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. Strength is equal bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLumbar spine X-ray obtained prior to the visit shows no acute bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nThe patient is a 65-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of lower back pain. He reports that he injured his lower back while moving boxes when his son was moving out of his college dorm. He states that he has been taking Tylenol and ibuprofen for pain. He reports that his symptoms have been ongoing for 4 months. He denies any incontinence and reports that he has had difficulty carrying heavy objects. He denies any fever or chills. He reports that he went to an urgent care center and they recommended an MRI. He was concerned about the cost of the MRI and has not had it done. He reports that he has tried physical therapy at his gym, but it did not help.\n\nPhysical examination reveals bony protuberance of C5-C6. There is no pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. Strength is equal bilaterally. X-rays of the lumbar spine obtained prior to the visit shows no acute bony abnormalities.\n\nLumbar spine pain.\n\nMedical Reasoning: I believe that the patient has a lumbar herniated disk. He reports ongoing symptoms of pain and numbness and tingling in the right foot. He denies any incontinence and reports difficulty with carrying heavy objects. His physical exam is also consistent with this diagnosis. His x-ray also shows no acute bony abnormalities.\n\nPatient Education and Counseling: I discussed the diagnosis, treatment, and prognosis with the patient today. I explained that his symptoms and physical exam are consistent with a lumbar herniated disk. I explained that I would like to start him on meloxicam 15 mg once a day and have him attend physical therapy. I also explained that I would like to order an MRI of the lumbar spine to get a better look at his condition. He has questions and was in agreement with the treatment plan.\n\nMedical Treatment: Physical therapy. Meloxicam 15 mg once a day.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 4 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that he had a total knee replacement 2 years ago.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient is a college student.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever or chills.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain or changes in bowel movements.\nGenitourinary: Denies urinary incontinence.\nNeurological: Reports right toe numbness.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 117/56 mmHg\nTemperature: 98.2°F\nHeart rate: 68\nOxygen saturation: 95%\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological\n- Neck: Bony protuberance of C5-6.\n- Lumbosacral: No pain to palpation. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine.\n\nMUSCULOSKELETAL\n- Lower extremities: Strength is equal bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLumbar spine x-ray, 2 views, obtained in office today, showed no acute bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Acute disk herniation.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient sustained this injury 4 months ago and has been trying physical therapy at his gym with no relief. He has also tried Tylenol, ibuprofen, heat, and ice without relief.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed that his symptoms are consistent with a disk herniation. He will undergo an MRI to further evaluate. We discussed treatment options for a disk herniation, including physical therapy, Meloxicam, and epidural steroid injections.\n\n2. Intervertebral disk disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: He had a total knee replacement 2 years ago.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed that his x-ray showed no acute bony abnormality.\n\n3. Knee status post total knee replacement.\n- Medical Reasoning: He had a total knee replacement 2 years ago.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed that his x-ray showed no acute bony abnormality.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 1 month. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJeremy Adams is a pleasant 57-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of his pain began 4 months ago, when he was moving boxes out of his son’s college dorm. At that time, he noted tingling in his right toes, which resolved after approximately 1 hour. After his injury, he felt as though he was unable to support his upper body weight. The patient reports weakness in his legs when carrying heavy objects. He reports a sharp pain in his lower back when he turns a certain way or rolls over in his sleep. The pain will resolve with repositioning. The patient was seen at urgent care and was referred to obtain an MRI, however he did not undergo this secondary to insurance issues. He has been utilizing Tylenol, ibuprofen, heat, and ice for pain, which does not provide relief. The patient notes the ibuprofen is slightly more beneficial than the Tylenol, however it does not relieve his symptoms. He denies upset stomach with the use of ibuprofen. Additionally, he attempted physical therapy at his gym without any relief. He denies any bladder or bowels issues, fevers, and chills.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient has a history of a right total knee arthroplasty 2 years ago, in spring of 2020. He states that his right knee feels 95% improved since the surgery.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers or chills.\nGastrointestinal: Denies bowel issues.\nGenitourinary: Denies bladder issues.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports lower back pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nTemperature: Normal.\nBlood pressure: 117/56 mm Hg.\nHeart rate: 68 BPM.\nOxygenation: 95% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNECK: Bony protuberance of the C5-C6. No pain to palpation of the C5-C6\nMSK: Examination of the Lumbar spine: No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. Strength is equal bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine were reviewed. These reveal no acute bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLow back pain, possible acute disc herniation.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. His x-rays did not reveal any signs of a fracture. I would like to obtain an MRI of the lumbar spine to evaluate for a possible acute disc herniation. I have prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg to treat his pain. He will continue with physical therapy and a referral was provided for this today.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
