PLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Harris is a 45-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. The onset of his pain began 3 days ago. The patient states he was playing soccer over the weekend and felt a pop in his right knee. He reports that he has swelling in the knee, but it has decreased since onset. The patient states that his knee is swollen and stiff, and he has been unable to fully extend or flex his right knee. He denies taking anything for pain other than anti-inflammatory medications. He is able to bear weight on the right leg but reports that it feels loose.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain, swelling, and decreased range of motion.\nSkin: Reports ecchymosis.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure, pulse, temperature, and respiratory rate were all within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK:\n- Examination of the right knee: Significant effusion present. Slight ecchymosis. Pain to palpation of the medial joint line. Lachman test positive. Decreased range of motion. Active and passive range of motion is decreased. Pain to flexion and extension. Muscle strength 5/5 bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right knee was obtained and reviewed today. No fractures or abnormalities were noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee pain, most consistent with an anterior cruciate ligament tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this point, I have discussed the diagnosis, treatment options, and the need for further evaluation with the patient. We have discussed the potential need for surgical intervention. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the right knee to further evaluate the integrity of the anterior cruciate ligament. We will re-evaluate after we have the results of the MRI. We discussed the importance of ice and anti-inflammatory medications. I have recommended that the patient wear a knee immobilizer and use crutches when walking. I have also recommended that the patient limit his acti