d the pain continues i\'m gon na go ahead and maybe order a cat scan and then at that point in time we can refer you to a pain doctor if we\'re still having significant pain\n[patient] sounds good\n[doctor] okay thank you i\'m gon na send my nursing to see you and we\'ll get you out the door\n[patient] thank you\n[doctor] take care\n[patient] that one is a follow-up mm-hmm\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLow back pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nNoah Brown is a pleasant 52-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain.\n\nThe onset of his back pain began 1 week ago. He denies any specific injury but notes that his pain is intermittent. He states that he has been taking over-the-counter medications without relief. The patient reports that his pain is located in the middle to the lower back and radiates to his left side. He notes that twisting or sudden movements to the left exacerbate his pain.\n\nThe patient has a history of diabetes and is currently taking metformin twice daily. He reports that he has been compliant with his medication regimen. He also reports that he tries to exercise regularly.\n\nThe patient also reports that he has been testing his blood sugar in the mornings, but occasionally he forgets. His blood sugar levels have been elevated recently. He denies that he is taking any other medications.\n\nThe patient states that he enjoys visiting a local bakery on the corner of Main Street. He reports that he tries to contain his sugar intake but notes that he often gets tempted.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure and heart rate were within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the lower extremities shows no pain or numbness or tingling in the feet.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the lumbar spine is pending.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Low back pain.\n2. Diabetes.\n\nPLAN\n\nI discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. For his low back pain, I believe he has a musculoskeletal strain of the lumbar region. I have ordered an x-ray of the lumbar spine which will be obtained as an outpatient. I have also referred him to physical therapy for pain and strengthening. I have prescribed him 400 mg of ibuprofen to be taken occasionally with Tylenol, up to 3 times a day, and I have given him a prescription for Flexeril 10 mg, to be taken once daily in the evening. He should avoid any strenuous or hazardous activities such as driving after taking the Flexeril.\n\nFor his diabetes, I believe it is under control. I have ordered an A1c to be checked as it has not been done in the last 5 months. He should continue with his diet and exercise and continue taking metformin. No changes are needed at this time.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks for further evaluation and treatment. He will follow up with his primary care physician for his diabetes. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nNoah Rodriguez is a pleasant 65-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of the pain began 1 week ago. He denies any injury to the back. He states that the pain has been continuous since the onset and has not resolved with over-the-counter medications. The pain is localized to the right and middle low back and radiates to the left. The pain is aggravated by sudden movements to the left. He denies numbness or tingling in his feet.\n\nThe patient has a history of diabetes. He is currently taking metformin twice daily. He denies any recent A1c tests. He states he tries to exercise and watch his diet. He is currently taking Tylenol with ibuprofen as needed for pain.\n\nThe patient denies any family history of diabetes.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient has a history of diabetes.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient has no history of surgery.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n