n with palpation of the right Achilles tendon. Pain with dorsiflexion of the right ankle.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAchilles tendinitis.\n\nPatient presents today for the evaluation of right heel pain. The onset of his pain began several months ago. The patient states that the pain is worse after activity and upon waking in the morning. He describes the pain as tightness and a tearing sensation. He states that the pain is alleviated with walking for about 10 to 15 minutes. The patient reports that he is training for a triathlon and he is concerned that his pain may be impacting his ability to focus on his training. He reports that he runs 10 kilometers in the morning, bikes for 45 minutes to an hour at lunch, and swims for 30 minutes to an hour in the evening. He states that he trains 5 days a week, alternating between long runs and long bike rides. He also swims on the other 5 days of the week. He denies stretching. He reports that he jumps on his bike and starts slow and then picks up speed as he warms up. He denies any medication use for his pain. He reports that he has not had any significant relief from his pain.\n\nAfter reviewing his examination today, I have found that the patient has achilles tendinitis. I have recommended that he stop training for a week or two. I have recommended that he take ibuprofen or advil, 3 tablets every 6 hours, as an anti-inflammatory. I have also recommended that he see physical therapy to work on strength and stretching. I have ordered a couple of physical therapy visits. I have recommended that he follow up in 7 to 10 days after his first couple of physical therapy appointments. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight heel pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nDennis Martin is a 63-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right heel pain. The patient is training for a triathlon and denies any prior history of this type of pain. The patient states that he has been running 10 km, biking for 45 minutes to an hour, and swimming for 30 minutes to an hour, 5 days a week. He alternates between long runs and long bike rides. He denies stretching and states that he does not have time for it. He also denies stretching after his workouts.\n\nThe patient reports pain in the back of his heel that radiates up the calf. He states that the pain is worse after he starts his workout and when he gets up in the morning. The pain is relieved after 10 to 15 minutes of walking. He reports that the pain returns when he sits for an extended period of time. He also reports pain after a lot of activity.\n\nThe patient denies any pain in the left leg.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the right leg: The Thompson test is negative. Palpation of the right calf is slightly tender. Palpation of the right heel is tender. Eversion and inversion of the foot causes pain. Compression of the right heel is painful.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nAchilles tendinitis, right heel.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and medical history today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regard to his current symptoms. I have recommended that the patient stop training for a week or two. I have recommended that he take ibuprofen or Advil 3 tablets every 6 hours. I also recommended that he attend 2 physical therapy visits. I will see the patient in 7 to 10 days after his first physical therapy visit. I have denied the patient an x-ray at this time.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 7 to 10 days after his first physical therapy appointment. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nDennis Martin is a pleasant 46-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right heel pain. The onset of his pain began 10 days ago while training for a triathlon. He locates his pain to the posterior aspect of his heel, which radiates proximally into his calf. His pain is aggravated by working out and when he first gets up in the morning. If he sits for a long period, his pain will return upon standing. The patient states that he runs 10 kilometers in the morning. At lunchtime, he tries to get on a bike for about 45 minutes to an hour. In the evening, he goes to the aquatic center and swims for 30 minutes to 1 hour. The patient states that he runs 5 days per week. He adds that he alternates between a long run and a long bike ride on weekends. He currently takes one day off from his exercises. The patient states that he does not stretch at all. He denies any left leg pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right heel pain. Denies left leg pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right heel: Pain with resisted extension. Mild pain with flexion. Positive calcaneal squeeze test. Severe tenderness to the Achilles tendon. Thompson test is negative, bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nNo new imaging was obtained today\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight Achilles tendinitis.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination today, I had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his symptoms are likely secondary to overuse. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient was instructed to take at least 1 week off from his training to recover from this. I will provide him with a referral for formal physical therapy to strengthen his right lower extremities. I have advised him to take 3 tablets of ibuprofen every 6 hours for pain. If his pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend obtaining an x-ray.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 7 to 10 days to check on his progress.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
