exam though there is a three over six systolic ejection murmur heard at the that i\'m hearing at the left base that\'s not like a huge deal especially considering like what you\'ve been through already in terms of the hospital but it is something that i wan na know in your chart when i listen to you your lungs your lungs are clear over here with your extremities i do notice you know some lower lower edema swelling sorry some lower swelling edema lower limb edema and it it is pitting one plus on your right radial artery the cath site is clean dry and intact without hematoma and i also your right radial artery pulses are palpable so i did review the results of your ekg and they show a normal sinus rhythm and a good r wave progression and a a evolutionary changes that are anticipated after a stemi one of the reasons though that we had you come in is that we noticed on your echo there is a reduced ejection fraction of thirty five percent we are a little bit concerned about that so let\'s talk about my assessment and plan for you your primary and like first diagnosis is gon na be coronary artery disease right you already have a history of that but you know we\'re gon na do some things to make we\'re gon na continue to evaluate that over time i want you to continue your aspirin eighty one milligrams daily and your brilinta ninety milligrams twice daily i wan na continue you on a high dose statin called lipitor and you\'re gon na be taking that eighty milligrams a day and we\'re gon na continue you on your toprol and that\'s gon na be fifty milligrams daily i am gon na refer you to cardiac rehab so we can get you some education and give you some confidence to get back to exercising regularly i know it was kinda scary to have such an episode but i promise like most of the patients i have love cardiac rehab and i think you\'ll do pretty well for your second diagnosis we have newly you have newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation what i think is like what that means is that you\'re you\'re pumping like the way that your heart is pumping is a bit concerning but i think like given your history that it will improve over time they got you into the cath lab pretty quickly so i think that the muscle is just kinda like stunned like surprised and since you\'re compliant with your meds i think you will recover we are gon na continue you on your lisinopril twenty milligrams daily however considering that you are retaining fluid i think that your third diagnosis is like we are crossing into acute heart failure i\'m gon na prescribe something called a diuretic it\'s called lasix and i do want to we need to take that forty milligrams once a day i wan na add aldactone twelve . five milligrams daily and i need you to get labs finally we will repeat another echo in about two months last but not least there is the hypertension your blood pressure seems fine i think with the aldactone that you\'ll tolerate that pretty well but we do want to maybe get you into some nutrition counseling to consider like what other diet options might be available to you maybe suggestions of things that you have n\'t thought about like cutting out meat especially any kind of preserved meat like pepperoni but maybe finding you some alternatives how does that sound\n[patient] that sounds good yeah so i\'m yeah as long as you tell me i\'m gon na get better i believe you\n[doctor] yes yeah i i strongly believe this is our best course of action and that you will get better given enough time i i see that you are making efforts i\'m really glad that you are out there walking and that you\'re cognizant of your diet and willing to make changes we will just you know monitor it over time okay\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] alright thank you\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up after interior ST-Elevation Myocardial Infarction (STEMI).\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJulia Anderson is a 65-year-old female who presents for follow-up after an interior ST-Elevation Myocardial Infarction (STEMI) status placement of intra-coronary artery stent in 2018. The patient has a history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and diabetes mellitus. The patient was previously seen approximately 6 months ago for a routine follow-up after her stent placement. At that time, she was asymptomatic. The patient reports that she has been walking her dog daily for the past 6 months. She has been compliant with her medications. The patient has been watching her diet but notes that she has not been as strict since her last heart attack. She enjoys pizza and tries. The patient denies chest pain or shortness of breath.\n\nThe patient has good insurance and has been able to afford her medications. The patient is currently employed and works full-time. She reports that she sleeps flat with the use of pillows. She is highly cooperative and is motivated to improve her health.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Reports leg swelling.\nGastrointestinal: Reports salt intake.\nEndocrine: Reports diabetes mellitus.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n- Carotid: No bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination of Extremities: 1+ pitting edema in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG shows normal sinus rhythm with good R wave progression and evolutionary changes anticipated after a STEM I.\n\nECHO shows a reduced ejection fraction of 35%.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is status post interior STEM I and has a history of coronary artery disease. She is currently asymptomatic.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of diet and exercise. I encouraged her to continue walking her dog. We discussed the potential benefits of cardiac rehabilitation.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue aspirin 81 mg daily. Continue Brilinta 90 mg BID. Continue Lipitor 80 mg daily. Continue Metoprolol 50 mg daily.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. She had a STEM I and is compliant with her medications.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the potential for improvement over time.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Acute (congestive) heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has leg swelling and a reduced ejection fraction.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of a low salt diet and fluid management.\n- Medical Treatment: Prescribed Lasix 40 mg daily. Prescribed Aldactone 12.5 mg daily. Ordered labs.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressure is currently well controlled.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the potential for blood pressure control with the addition of Aldactone.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue current medications.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 months for an echocardiogram.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 months for an echocardiogram. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up after acute coronary syndrome.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJulia Smith is a pleasant 52-year-old female who presents for a follow-up appointment today after being discharged from the hospital with a diagnosis of acute coronary syndrome. The patient was last seen in clinic approximately 6 months ago for a right coronary artery stent placement. She denies any chest pain or shortness of breath. She has been walking her dog daily, going approximately 1.5 to 2 miles. She is excited for fall and the upcoming holiday. She denies lying flat at night, but does use 2 pillows. She has noticed lower extremity swelling since discharge from th