r an interior ST-Elevation Myocardial Infarction (STEMI) status placement of intra-coronary artery stent in 2018. The patient has a history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and diabetes mellitus. The patient was previously seen approximately 6 months ago for a routine follow-up after her stent placement. At that time, she was asymptomatic. The patient reports that she has been walking her dog daily for the past 6 months. She has been compliant with her medications. The patient has been watching her diet but notes that she has not been as strict since her last heart attack. She enjoys pizza and tries. The patient denies chest pain or shortness of breath.\n\nThe patient has good insurance and has been able to afford her medications. The patient is currently employed and works full-time. She reports that she sleeps flat with the use of pillows. She is highly cooperative and is motivated to improve her health.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Reports leg swelling.\nGastrointestinal: Reports salt intake.\nEndocrine: Reports diabetes mellitus.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n- Carotid: No bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination of Extremities: 1+ pitting edema in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG shows normal sinus rhythm with good R wave progression and evolutionary changes anticipated after a STEM I.\n\nECHO shows a reduced ejection fraction of 35%.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is status post interior STEM I and has a history of coronary artery disease. She is currently asymptomatic.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of diet and exercise. I encouraged her to continue walking her dog. We discussed the potential benefits of cardiac rehabilitation.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue aspirin 81 mg daily. Continue Brilinta 90 mg BID. Continue Lipitor 80 mg daily. Continue Metoprolol 50 mg daily.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. She had a STEM I and is compliant with her medications.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the potential for improvement over time.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Acute (congestive) heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has leg swelling and a reduced ejection fraction.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of a low salt diet and fluid management.\n- Medical Treatment: Prescribed Lasix 40 mg daily. Prescribed Aldactone 12.5 mg daily. Ordered labs.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressure is currently well controlled.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the potential for blood pressure control with the addition of Aldactone.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue current medications.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 months for an echocardiogram.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 months for an echocardiogram. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up after acute coronary syndrome.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJulia Smith is a pleasant 52-year-old female who presents for a follow-up appointment today after being discharged from the hospital with a diagnosis of acute coronary syndrome. The patient was last seen in clinic approximately 6 months ago for a right coronary artery stent placement. She denies any chest pain or shortness of breath. She has been walking her dog daily, going approximately 1.5 to 2 miles. She is excited for fall and the upcoming holiday. She denies lying flat at night, but does use 2 pillows. She has noticed lower extremity swelling since discharge from the hospital. She is compliant with her medications and has been able to afford them. She is also compliant with her diet and is trying to watch her salt intake, but notes that she loves pizza. She has been managing her diabetes with metformin and continues to be active by walking her dog.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nNeurological: Denies leg swelling.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck: No jugular venous distention.\nCV: 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base. No carotid bruits.\nRespiratory: Clear bilaterally.\nMSK: 1+ pitting edema in the lower extremities. Radial artery pulses are palpable.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG shows normal sinus rhythm, good R wave progression, and evolutionary changes expected after a STEMI.\nEcho shows a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. There is moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient had a recent acute coronary syndrome with a stent placement in the RCA.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: She was advised that this is a chronic condition that will be managed with medication and lifestyle modifications.\n- Medical Treatment: She will continue her aspirin 81 mg daily, Brilinta 90 mg twice daily, Lipitor 80 mg daily, and Toprol 50 mg daily.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient had an ejection fraction of 35% on her echocardiogram today.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her that this may recover over time given her recent cardiac catheterization and that she is compliant with her medications.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient had moderate mitral regurgitation on her echocardiogram today.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her that this is likely due to her recent cardiac catheterization and that she is compliant with her medications.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue.\n\n4. Heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and diabetes and has recently had an acute coronary syndrome and stent placement.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her that this is acute and that we will manage it with medication.\n- Medical Treatment: Start Lasix 40 mg daily. Add Aldactone 12.5 mg daily.\n\n5. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressure seems fine today.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her that we will continue to monitor it and that she will be compliant with her medications.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 months for a repeat echocardiogram. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up status post inferior STEMI.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nRCA stent 2018\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\naspirin 81 mg daily\nBrilinta 90 mg twice daily\nLipitor 80 mg daily\nToprol 50 mg daily\nlisinopril 20 mg daily\nmetformin\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral leg swelling.\n\nVITALS\n\nWithin normal limits\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: No carotid bruits. A 3/6 systolic ejection murmur is heard at the left base.\n- Examination: Right radial artery cath site is clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. Radial artery pulses are palpable.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Bilateral lower limb edema. Pitting 1+.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG is reviewed and demonstrates a normal sinus rhythm. Good R wave progression. Evolutionary changes that are anticipated after a STEMI.\n\nEchocardiogram is reviewed and demonstrates a reduced ejection fraction of 35%.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of coronary artery disease that we will continue to monitor.\n- Medical Treatment: She will continue aspirin 81 mg daily and Brilinta 90 mg twice daily. She will continue Lipitor 80 mg a day and Toprol 50 mg daily. I am going to refer her to cardiac rehab for education and to gain confidence to resume exercising regularly.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: A recent echocardiogram revealed a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. She was able to be treated in the cath lab in a timely manner, so I think that the muscle is just stunned. Given her history. it will improve over time.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that she will recover since she is compliant with her medications.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Acute heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is retaining fluid.\n- Medical Treatment: I am going to prescribe Lasix 40 mg once a day. I want to add Aldactone 12.5 mg daily. I will order lab work. We will obtain a repeat echo in approximately 2 months.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: Blood pressure is well controlled. She will tolerate the Aldactone well.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue exercising and to be cognizant of her diet. I explained that there are healthier alternatives available to reduce the amount of preserved meats she consumes.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily. The patient received a referral to nutrition counseling to consider what other diet options might be available to her.\n\n5. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: Stable.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue monitoring her blood glucose levels.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue metformin as prescribed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up as instructed.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
