e you on the flovent that seems to be doing pretty well continue to take that daily and then also albuterol as needed so how does that sound for a plan any other questions comments suggestions\n[patient] that sounds good and i also feel like i\'m eating been eating a lot of spicy food\n[doctor] hmmm\n[patient] lately that could be contributing towards my gastritis so i\'m gon na cut back on that as well\n[doctor] okay yeah i\'d also recommend while this is hurting you definitely cut back on the spices the spicy food easier said than done sometimes i know but yep yep alright sounds good well listen i\'ll see you back in four weeks give me a holler if if you have any questions or anything else in the meantime\n[patient] i will\n[doctor] alright you take care sure you got\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes type 2 and asthma.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she is a coffee drinker. She states she has been drinking a lot of coffee lately.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports she is taking metformin 500 mg once a day. She notes that last visit, the dose was increased to 500 mg twice a day.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Endorses abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting. Endorses increased pain with exercise. Endorses increased pain with caffeine. Endorses dyspepsia.\nMusculoskeletal: Endorses increased abdominal pain with exercise.\nRespiratory: Endorses wheezing. Denies asthma attacks.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory: Mild expiratory wheezes.\nCardiovascular: Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur.\nGastrointestinal: Mild epigastric tenderness. Soft abdomen. No tenderness in the right lower quadrant. No significant tenderness in the right upper quadrant.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAcute gastritis.\n• Medical Reasoning: I believe this is related to her caffeine intake and alcohol use.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to cut back on caffeine and alcohol.\n• Medical Treatment: I will prescribe Zantac 150 mg twice a day.\n\nDiabetes type 2.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well with her blood sugars.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue metformin. Increase dose slightly. Morning dose to 1000 mg. Evening dose to 500 mg.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: Continue to check blood sugars. Let me know when she comes back.\n\nAsthma.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well with her asthma.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Flovent. Continue Albuterol as needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nFollow up in 4 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes type 2 and asthma.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of cholecystectomy.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she has been drinking alcohol heavily and consuming a lot of coffee.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nHENT: Denies shortness of breath.\nGastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain and nausea and vomiting.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports abdominal pain with exercise.\n\nVITALS\n\nHeart rate 75, regular rhythm. Blood pressure 125/75. Respiratory rate 16. Temperature 98.1 degrees Fahrenheit.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: EAD bilaterally. Mild expiratory wheezes.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft. Mild tenderness in the epigastrium. No tenderness in the RLQ. No significant tenderness in the RUQ.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Abdominal pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with abdominal pain. She has a history of diabetes and asthma. She has been drinking alcohol heavily and consuming a lot of coffee. She reports that her blood sugars have been under control.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed her symptoms and I recommended that she cut back on her alcohol and caffeine intake. She will start taking Zantac twice a day. We also discussed her diabetes and I recommended that she continue to check her blood sugars.\n- Medical Treatment: Prescribing Zantac 150 mg twice a day.\n- Additional Testing: Urinalysis, urine pregnancy test, CBC, CMP.\n\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well with her diabetes. She has been taking metformin.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to continue checking her blood sugars.\n- Medical Treatment: Increasing the morning dose of metformin to 1000 mg. Continuing the evening dose at 500 mg.\n\n3. Asthma.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well with her asthma. She has been using Flovent and albuterol as needed.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to continue using Flovent daily and albuterol as needed.\n- Medical Treatment: Continuing Flovent 44 mcg twice a day.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 4 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes type 2, asthma, and gastritis.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of moderately heavy alcohol use. She also enjoys coffee.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin 500 mg twice daily, Flovent twice daily, and albuterol as-needed.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever.\nGastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting. Denies diarrhea.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Mild end expiratory wheezes, very faint.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from prior exam.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Mild tenderness in the epigastrium. Abdomen is soft. No tenderness in the 