ust the medication in the future alright so do you have any questions for me\n[patient] no sounds good\n[doctor] alright so we will see you next time and my nurse will be in with those prescriptions\n[patient] alright thank you\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nPhilip Smith is a 64-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. The patient states that he hurt his elbow about a week ago while playing lacrosse with his kids. He denies falling. He states that the pain is located at the tip of his elbow and radiates down his forearm. He describes the pain as sharp and states that it occurs with movement. He denies taking any medications for the pain. He states that he tried taking Advil a couple of days ago, but he usually just forgets to take it.\n\nThe patient has a past medical history of hypertension and diabetes. He is currently on 20 mg of lisinopril for his hypertension. He states that he takes this medication daily. He is also on 500 mg of metformin for his diabetes. He states that he is not currently taking insulin. He denies checking his blood sugars regularly. He states that when he does check his blood sugars, they are usually between 80 to 100.\n\nThe patient states that he has been compliant with a low salt diet. He states that he avoids adding salt to his food and that the whole family follows a Mediterranean diet.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nHypertension\nDiabetes\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that he plays lacrosse with his kids.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nLisinopril 20 mg.\nMetformin 500 mg.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever, chills, or weight loss.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath or cough.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\nSkin: Denies rashes or lesions.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 150/90 mmHg\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\nLungs are clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\nGrade 2 out of 6 systolic ejection murmur.\n\nMusculoskeletal\nExamination of the right elbow: Pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension. Normal range of motion.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow: No fracture or bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow contusion.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is having pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. He has a normal range of motion. He denies falling. He is having pain with movement. He has a bruise on his elbow.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the diagnosis with the patient today. I advised him that this is a contusion. I advised him to ice his elbow three times a day for 20 minutes at a time. I advised him to take 800 mg of ibuprofen twice a day.\n- Medical Treatment: We discussed treatment options. I prescribed 800 mg of ibuprofen, oral, twice a day. I advised him to ice his elbow three times a day for 20 minutes at a time.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is on 20 mg of lisinopril. His blood pressure today was 150/90.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed his blood pressure today. I advised him to continue taking his lisinopril.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg.\n\n3. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is on 500 mg of metformin. His blood sugar levels are between 80 to 100.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised him to continue taking his metformin.\n- Additional Testing: We will order a hemoglobin A1c.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. We will obtain a hemoglobin A1c. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient has a past medical history of hypertension and diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient is an avid lacrosse player.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills or changes in weight.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea on exertion.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\nHENT: Denies headaches or vision changes.\nNeurological: Denies numbness, tingling or weakness.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination of the Right Elbow: Pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension. Normal range of motion.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow, 3 views, obtained in office today is unremarkable.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow contusion.\n- Medical Reasoning: I believe the patient was struck with a lacrosse stick and sustained a contusion to the right elbow.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of his injury and the treatment options. I advised him to ice the area 3 times a day for 20 minutes at a time and to take 800 mg of ibuprofen twice a day for pain and swelling. I also advised him to avoid playing lacrosse with his son for the next couple of weeks.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressure was elevated today, 150/90.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue taking his lisinopril 20 mg daily and to continue his low salt diet. I also advised him to monitor his blood pressure regularly.\n\n3. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been compliant with his metformin 500 mg daily and his blood sugars have been stable.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised him to continue taking his metformin 500 mg daily and to check his blood sugars regularly. I also advised him to continue his low salt diet. We will obtain an A1c today to check his levels and see if we need to adjust the medication.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 150/90 mmHg\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\nGrade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension but otherwise normal range of motion. There is swelling about the elbow, as well as mild ecchymosis.\n\nHematology/Lymphatic/Immunology\n- Examination: Bruising noted over the right elbow.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow was reviewed and demonstrates no evidence of fracture or bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow contusion.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient sustained an injury to his elbow when his son struck him with a lacrosse stick. His x-ray and exam findings are consistent with an elbow contusion.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to avoid playing lacrosse for the next couple of weeks, just until his elbow heals. After that, he should be fine to continue playing normally.\n- Medical Treatment: I\'m going to prescribe him ibuprofen 800 mg twice daily to help with some of the pain and swelling. He should also ice the area in 20-minute intervals 3 times per day to further reduce his symptoms.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to be well-controlled with dietary modifications and lisinopril.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged him to keep up with his dietary modifications.\n- Medical Treatment: We will keep him on lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Diabetes.\n- Medical 