}}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nElevated cholesterol.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports being an avid biker, enjoys spending time outdoors.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that she has a cousin who died at the age of 44 or 45. She believes that he had a heart attack. Patient also reports that she has a good relationship with her grandparents, who she has asked about her cholesterol levels in the past. She does not believe that they have a history of high cholesterol.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or changes in weight.\nHENT: Denies sore throat.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath or changes in cough.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies changes in bowel movements or appetite.\nGenitourinary: Denies changes in urination.\nPsychiatric: Denies changes in sleep.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure is normal.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: No focal neurologic deficits.\nCardiovascular: No murmur, rub, or gallop.\nRespiratory: Clear lungs.\nEyes: No jugular venous distension.\nNeck: No thyroid enlargement.\nHent: No lymphadenopathy.\nGastrointestinal: No hepatosplenomegaly.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCholesterol level is elevated at 210.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Hypercholesterolemia.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presented with an elevated cholesterol level.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of diet and exercise in maintaining a healthy cholesterol level. I advised the patient to follow a well balanced diet that includes a variety of foods that are low in saturated fats and sugars. I also advised her to continue her current exercise routine. I also advised her to follow up with a nutritionist.\n• Medical Treatment: We will order a full cholesterol panel, glucose, complete metabolic panel, thyroid studies, and liver panels. We will also perform genetic testing to determine if this is a familial or secondary cause. We will fo