ded to help with the pain.\n- I have also recommended that the patient undergo physical therapy. The physical therapist will show her a number of lower extremity exercises to help increase her lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSophia Rios is a pleasant 24-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. The onset of the pain was approximately 6 months ago and the patient cannot recall a specific event that caused the pain. The pain is deep achy and localized to the back of the kneecaps. The patient describes hearing creaking in her knees. The patient reports that the pain is worse when getting up from a seated position. She works from home and reports that the pain is worse when she is getting up from her desk. The patient reports that she is able to run 5 to 6 miles per day, but states that she has decreased her mileage to half a mile due to the pain. She denies any swelling or redness in the knees. The patient reports that she has never injured her knees before and denies any history of bone fractures. She has never had any significant knee trauma. She has been running outdoors and on a treadmill. She has been running in Boston and enjoys running near the harbor. She has never broken a bone before. She broke her arm in 6th grade.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nIbuprofen and Aleve.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain.\nNeurological: Denies numbness or tingling.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nHENT: Denies headaches.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK\n- Neurovascular intact distally.\n- Strength: 3/5 for abduction of 